        ans: 2,
        exp: "Rule: Vicarious liability under respondeat superior requires a master-servant (employer-employee) or principal-agent relationship, where the tortfeasor acts on behalf of the principal and subject to the principal's control[cite: 28]. In a motor vehicle test-drive, the relationship between a car dealership and a prospective purchaser is that of bailor and bailee, not principal and agent[cite: 28]. The customer is driving for his own personal purpose of evaluating the vehicle, not conducting business on behalf of the dealer[cite: 28]. In the absence of an owner-consent statute or negligent entrustment (neither of which is present here), a commercial vehicle owner is not vicariously liable at common law for the ordinary negligence of a test-driving prospective buyer (Option C)[cite: 28]. Option A is incorrect because the mere physical presence of a salesperson in the passenger seat to provide directions and answer questions does not transform a test-drive into an agency relationship where the dealer exercises legal control over driving mechanics[cite: 28]. Option B is incorrect because a test-drive is an arm's-length negotiation between potential seller and buyer, not a joint venture sharing a common business purpose and joint control[cite: 28]. Option D is incorrect because vicarious liability applies broadly across all employment relationships, not merely freight carriers[cite: 28]."
